+Landscape 3:2. The story opening: inside a crude Karaveen nano-infirmary, a makeshift clinic assembled from salvaged medical and industrial hardware inside a sandstone room. A badly injured survivor (adult, gender-ambiguous, face partly bandaged, head wrapped, one eye showing a fine metallic ocular implant ring) lies on a reclined salvaged surgical bed. Attached to their forearm is an ancient, worn wrist-mounted computational device of dark bronze and ceramic with thin cables running to the bed; it emits a faint cyan glow as it boots. Two Karaveen doctors in layered dust-coloured robes, goggles pushed up, gloved hands, watch tensely; a small articulated nanite injector arm hangs from a gantry. Warm low lamp light, dust in a sunbeam from a high window, cool cyan accent only from the device and a diagnostic glass panel showing abstract waveform shapes (no readable text). Mood: fragile survival, quiet tension, wonder. Mysterious but not horror.
+
+Original cinematic science-fiction concept art for the game Ashfall, set on the desert planet Tir. Painterly-but-photoreal AAA key-art finish, natural film lighting, believable human scale and construction. Palette: warm sun-baked sandstone and mineral stone, varied weathered salvaged industrial metal (painted, bare, rusted), patched canvas and cloth accents, localized oasis greens, cool sky/shade, and only RESTRAINED cyan technology glow. Tone: lived-in humanity, survival and rebuilding, quiet wonder — not a generic robot war, not grimdark. Hard constraints: no text, no lettering, no logos, no watermarks, no UI captions, no signatures, no borders; no recognisable real-world brands or existing game IP; anatomically correct people with correct hands; no extra limbs.

